<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-tangent-vectors" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Tangent Vectors</title>

  <p>
    Given a curve <m>\vec{r}(t)</m>, we can plot a secant vector by computing 
    <m>\vec{r}(t+h)-\vec{r}(t)</m> for some real number <m>h</m>.

    <figure xml:id="fig-sec-vector">
      <image>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-secant-vector">
          <xi:include href="prefigure/secant-vector.xml"/>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
      <caption>A secant vector to a parametric curve</caption>
    </figure>
    Intuitively, we might consider constructing a <term>tangent vector</term>
    through some sort of limiting process as <m>h \rightarrow 0</m>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Recall the definition of a limit from single variable calculus:
  </p>
  <definition xml:id="def-limit">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>f(x)</m> be a real-valued function defined on an open interval near a point <m>a</m>
        (except possibly at <m>a</m>).  Then we say that <m>\displaystyle \lim _{x \rightarrow a} f(x)=L</m>
        if for every <m>\epsilon \gt 0</m>, there exists <m>\delta \gt 0</m> such that 
        <md>|f(x)-L| \lt \epsilon \text{ whenever } |x-a| \lt \delta</md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    In other words, <m>\displaystyle \lim _{x \rightarrow a}f(x)=L</m> means that for
    every choice of <m>\epsilon \gt 0</m>, we can 
    ensure that <m>f(x)</m> is within <m>\epsilon</m> of <m>L</m> by choosing
    a sufficiently small interval around <m>a</m>.
  </p>
  <p>
    To adapt this to vector valued functions, we simply need to make precise what it means
    to be <q>within <m>\epsilon</m> of a vector</q>.  
  </p>

  <definition> 
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\vec{L} \in \R^n</m>, and let <m>\epsilon \gt 0</m>.  Then we set
        <md>B_{\epsilon}(\vec{L}) = \left\{ \vec{x} \in \R^n \ \middle| \  |\vec{x}-\vec{L}| \lt \epsilon \right\}</md>,
        i.e. so that <m>B_\epsilon (\vec{L})</m> is the set of all points in <m>\R^n</m> that 
        are within <m>\epsilon</m> of the point <m>\vec{L}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <definition xml:id="def-limit-vector">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> be a vector-valued function defined on an open interval near a point <m>a</m>
        (except possibly at <m>a</m>).  Then we say that <m>\displaystyle \lim _{t \rightarrow a} \vec{r}(t)=\vec{L}</m>
        if for every <m>\epsilon \gt 0</m>, there exists <m>\delta \gt 0</m> such that 
        <md>\vec{r}(t) \in B_\epsilon(\vec{L}) \text{ whenever } |t-a| \lt \delta</md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>While conceptually nice, this definition is difficult to work with in practice.  Fortunately,
    we are able to leverage what we know about single variable limits.
  </p>

  <proposition xml:id="prop-limit-component">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle x(t),y(t),z(t)</m> be a vector valued function.
        Then 
        <md>\lim _{t \rightarrow a} \vec{r}(t) = \left \langle \lim _{t \rightarrow a} x(t), \lim _{t \rightarrow a} y(t), \lim _{t \rightarrow a} z(t) \right \rangle</md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </proposition>

  <p>With this in hand, we can now return to our idea of a tangent vector.</p>

  <definition xml:id="def-tangent-vector">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> be a vector-valued function.  The <term>tangent vector</term> to 
        <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> is the limit
        <md>\vec{r}^\prime(t) = \lim _{h \rightarrow 0} \frac{\vec{r}(t+h)-\vec{r}(t)}{h}</md>.
      </p>
      <p>When <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> represents the position of an object, the tangent vector <m>\vec{r}^\prime(t)</m>
        is also called the <term>velocity (vector)</term>.  
        Its magnitude, <m>|\vec{r}^\prime(t)|</m> is called <term>speed</term>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>An immediate consequence of <xref ref="prop-limit-component"/> is
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p> If <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle x(t), y(t), z(t)\rangle</m>, then
        <md>\vec{r}^\prime(t) = \langle x^\prime(t), y^\prime(t), z^\prime(t) \rangle</md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </proposition>

  <activity>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Find the tangent vector to each of the following curves.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\vec{r}_1(t)= \langle \cos(t), \sin(t) \rangle</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\vec{r}_2(t)= \langle -\sin(t), \cos(t) \rangle</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\vec{r}_3(t)= \langle t^3, t^2 \rangle</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <p>Because derivatives can be computed component-wise, our usual derivative rules
    translate to the vector context in a straightforward way.
  </p>
  <fact>
    <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>f</m> be a differentiable real-valued function of a real variable <m>t</m>. Let <m>\vec{r}</m> and <m>\vec{s}</m> be
      differentiable vector-valued functions of the real variable <m>t</m>. Then
      <dl>
        <li>
          <title>Sum Rule</title>
          <p>
            <m>\frac{d}{dt} \left[\vec{r}(t) + \vec{s}(t) \right] = \vec{r}\, '(t) + \vec{s}\, '(t)</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Scalar Multiplication Product Rule</title>
          <p>
            <m>\frac{d}{dt} \left[f(t) \vec{r}(t)\right] = f(t) \vec{r}\, '(t) + f'(t) \vec{r}(t)</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Dot Product Rule</title>
          <p>
            <m>\frac{d}{dt} \left[\vec{r}(t) \cdot \vec{s}(t) \right] = \vec{r}\, '(t) \cdot \vec{s}(t) + \vec{r}(t) \cdot \vec{s}\, '(t)</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Cross Product Rule</title>
          <p>
            <m>\frac{d}{dt} \left[\vec{r}(t) \times \vec{s}(t) \right] = \vec{r}\, '(t)  \times \vec{s}(t) + \vec{r}(t) \times \vec{s}\, '(t)</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Chain Rule</title>
          <p>
            <m>\frac{d}{dt} \left[\vec{r}\left(f(t)\right)\right] = f'(t)  \  \vec{r}\, '(f(t))</m>
          </p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </p>
    </statement>
  </fact>


</section>